WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.259 --> 00:00:02.842
(lively music)

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.120
- Naturally, in the wine industry,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.020
we have good years, we have bad years.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.410
Last year, we had the fires.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.950
Major fires here in
California, in Napa Valley,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.950
and then this is the
second year of drought.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:21.340
So it's been rough just from that aspect.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.631
It is certainly difficult times.

00:00:23.631 --> 00:00:26.450
Not only we have labor shortages

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.300
but at the same time,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.810
there's scarcity of supply,

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.560
and there's an abundance of demand.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.900
- [Narrator] Business owners,
like winemaker Malek Amrani,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.460
are facing three main pressures

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.480
on how they price their products.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.980
High demand, rising costs of labor,

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.180
and rising costs of supplies.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.910
Whether Amrani and others
like him absorb these costs

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.560
or pass them on to consumers

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.431
determines the course of
inflation in the US economy.

00:00:51.431 --> 00:00:55.260
- The US economy is made
up of literally hundreds

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.150
of millions of individual workers,

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.990
businesses and employers.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.540
And inflation is really the output

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.870
of what all those hundreds

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.440
of millions of people, tons basically

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.180
are deciding in the
transactions between each other,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.625
and their own decisions.

00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:11.080
(tractor rumbling)

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.260
- [Narrator] These decisions
have big consequences

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.790
for business owners.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.410
Eating the rising costs they're facing

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.230
could destroy their profit margin

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.743
but passing them on could
drive customers away.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.463
- So this is the equivalent
of a glass of wine.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.140
The Vice Wine is a mid-sized
Napa Valley winery.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.840
We produce about 25,000 cases a year.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.858
Typically we are a little bit

00:01:33.858 --> 00:01:37.210
under what the typical price point

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.050
for a Napa Valley wine will be.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.080
The house tier,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.030
we are between $22 and 33 bucks.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.420
For our single vineyard reserve tier,

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.623
that starts at $38 and it
goes all the way up to 69.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.520
- [Narrator] Like many
businesses right now,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.930
Amrani says he's
experiencing strong demand

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:56.973
for his product.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:01.043
But he's facing a lot of
obstacles to meet that demand.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.740
- We are in a perfect storm

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.660
where kinda all the elements,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.780
things that we never had to worry about

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.773
are becoming really difficult to source.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.200
It's been very hard to source labor

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.424
since the start of the pandemic.

00:02:18.424 --> 00:02:20.070
(grapes rolling)

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.110
It's kind of getting harder this year.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.010
The $15 minimum wage really kind

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.200
of doesn't apply to Napa

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.630
because we've been paying
here 20 to $22 pre-pandemic.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.200
And now since we can't find labor,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.560
we are in the upper 20s to lower 30s

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.933
just for basic vineyard
labor and seller labor.

00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:44.540
(glass rattling)

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.373
- [Narrator] Glass has been

00:02:45.373 --> 00:02:46.970
particularly difficult to source.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.070
- I will say generic glass mold

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.070
that used to take me a text message,

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.360
maybe a phone call or an email to order,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.313
now takes me 20 to 40
hours on average to find.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.090
I have so many of them.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.070
Wine glass suppliers.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.140
It's like you've gotta be talking

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.653
to 12 people all the freaking time.

00:03:08.811 --> 00:03:10.700
(phone ringing)

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.750
- [Cynthia] Thank you for
calling West Coast Bottles.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:13.830
How are you?

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.804
- Hey, I'm good, Cynthia, how you been?

00:03:15.804 --> 00:03:18.829
- [Cynthia] I don't have
any good news for you today.

00:03:18.829 --> 00:03:20.170
- Oh shoot.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.407
- [Cynthia] How much are
you selling this wine for?

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:25.847
You need to raise the price.

00:03:25.847 --> 00:03:27.680
- 29 bucks.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.840
And then when I do find the glass,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.240
after spending a long time looking for it,

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.483
then I'm paying 30 to 50% more.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:41.600
Bringing a container out of
China of glass a year ago

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.230
would have been somewhere
around 3 to $4,000.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.180
Today we're north of 12 to $15,000.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.360
She wouldn't even tell me
the price on the phone.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.300
That's how bad it is.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.630
I wouldn't be surprised
if she doubles the price.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.793
She's saying it's all
because of the shipping.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:03.380
Even cardboard's been hard to source.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.690
I didn't expect that the
cardboard would be an issue,

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.630
especially generic craft cardboard

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.280
that doesn't have anything printed on it.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.650
The prices dramatically went up.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.093
No less than 50% in some instances.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:19.270
The only thing that may
help ease the difficulties

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:22.290
on the supply chain is lower demand,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.520
and I really don't see
the demand slowing down

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:25.373
any time soon.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:27.820
- So we know that a big part

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.884
of the inflation process is psychological.

00:04:30.884 --> 00:04:33.620
Inflation will partly depend

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.350
what people think it will be.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:36.250
So think of the business

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.623
that's facing an increase in its costs.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.110
It could either decide all right,

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.870
these higher costs are here to stay,

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.170
and I'm gonna raise the
price for my customers

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.940
or he can decide I've
got a pretty good sense

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.260
of this temporary disruption,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.280
and those costs are going
back a year from now.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.287
If I raise my prices now,

00:04:53.287 --> 00:04:55.430
I'm going to lose market share,

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.660
and it'll be for nothing

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:59.260
because those costs will
go down a year from now.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.530
So he's got to make that call.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.250
- We're absolutely trying
to swallow the cost

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.940
and just deal with it

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:12.297
but because it's not a 5%
or 2% or 10% inflation,

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.250
in many cases, it's 40% up,

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.370
it will certainly have consequences

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.133
when it comes to price increases.

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:25.530
Our brand was born on the idea

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.270
of making Napa Valley accessible

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:28.740
for that everyday occasion.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.660
If we are forced to raise
the prices in the house tier,

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.980
it's almost like we're
in a different territory,

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.270
appealing to a different consumer.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.970
A lot of our fans of The Vice Wine,

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.690
I can see them remaining loyal to us

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:43.830
but for others, they may just not be able

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.160
to afford that price increase

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.620
and they may look at other regions

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.180
besides Napa Valley.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.920
Maybe go to a different wine region

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:54.716
or even international wines
versus the local wines.

00:05:54.716 --> 00:05:57.383
(playful music)

